---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/uv-lamps-for-vitamin-d`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'UV Lamps for Vitamin D',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'UV Lamps for Vitamin D', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="UV Lamps for Vitamin D: What Actually Works and What Doesn't"
  description="Only UVB in the 290–315 nm range makes vitamin D — not SAD lamps, not tanning beds by default. Wavelengths, dosing, and why supplements are usually the smarter choice."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/uv-lamps-for-vitamin-d", label: "UV Lamps for Vitamin D" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">UV Lamps for Vitamin D</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Vitamin D synthesis requires ultraviolet-B in a narrow wavelength window
      (290–315 nm, with peak activity around 297 nm). Most consumer "sun lamps" and
      almost all "SAD lamps" emit no meaningful UVB and produce zero vitamin D. Only
      specific medical-grade or tanning-industry UVB sources do the job.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The wavelength requirement</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>UVA (315–400 nm)</strong> — most tanning beds emit primarily UVA. Produces a tan, ages skin, does NOT produce vitamin D.</li>
      <li><strong>UVB (280–315 nm)</strong> — the vitamin D-synthesising band. Only UVB in the 290–315 nm range converts 7-dehydrocholesterol to previtamin D3.</li>
      <li><strong>Narrowband UVB (311–313 nm)</strong> — used in dermatology for psoriasis and vitiligo; produces vitamin D as a side effect but is not marketed for that purpose.</li>
      <li><strong>Full-spectrum "sun lamps" and SAD lamps</strong> — these use visible-white LED light around 10,000 lux; no UVB, no vitamin D. They treat seasonal affective disorder by circadian entrainment, not by making vitamin D.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Consumer options that do produce vitamin D</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Medical UVB units</strong> (Sperti, Solarc RMR-1600 series) — engineered to emit 290–315 nm. Sessions of a few minutes, 3× per week, can raise 25(OH)D similarly to oral supplementation. FDA-registered but not covered by insurance for vitamin D indication.</li>
      <li><strong>Tanning beds</strong> — vary hugely. Some newer beds include UVB tubes; older/most beds are UVA-dominant. Deliberate tanning-bed use for vitamin D is not recommended by any professional dermatology body because of melanoma risk.</li>
      <li><strong>Reptile UVB lamps</strong> (10.0 or 12% UVB fluorescents designed for chameleons and lizards) — do produce UVB and have been used off-label for vitamin D. Not FDA-cleared for human use; exposure control is up to you; skin cancer risk of chronic use is unstudied.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When lamps actually make sense</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Severe fat malabsorption</strong> — cystic fibrosis, short-bowel, post-bariatric — where oral cholecalciferol fails despite high doses.</li>
      <li><strong>Established rickets or osteomalacia</strong> when supplementation has failed.</li>
      <li><strong>Concurrent psoriasis or vitiligo</strong> where narrowband UVB is already being used therapeutically.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">For everyone else — supplements win</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      An OTC cholecalciferol capsule is cheaper, safer, and more precise than any UV
      device. UVB carries carcinogenic risk (basal cell, squamous cell, and — with UVA —
      melanoma), needs eye protection, and requires uncovered skin at a specific distance
      for measured minutes. For 95%+ of people asking about "vitamin D lamps," the correct
      answer is: don't buy the lamp, buy the supplement.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Recreational tanning-bed use is a proven
      melanoma risk factor — do not rebrand it as "vitamin D therapy" for yourself.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/uv-lamps-for-vitamin-d" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
